    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>If your wind and hail coverage is with the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA), you must file within one year of the date of damage. If it's a private homeowners or commercial policy, the deadline is whatever your policy says. Many private Texas policies also use a one-year window for reporting wind and hail damage, but the wording varies, so you need to read your own policy rather than rely on a rule of thumb.</p>
<p>Three separate clocks matter: the deadline to <em>report</em> the damage, the deadline to <em>sue</em> if the claim goes wrong, and the deadlines the insurer has to <em>respond</em> once you've filed. This article covers all three, plus the practical problem that catches most late claims: proving when the hail fell.</p>

<h2>TWIA: one year from the date of damage</h2>
<p>TWIA covers wind and hail for properties in designated coastal counties where private carriers often exclude it. Its deadline is set by statute, not by the policy alone.</p>
<div class="callout callout--law"><p><strong>Texas law:</strong> a TWIA claim must be filed within one year of the date the damage occurred. The commissioner may extend that deadline by up to 180 days for good cause. Once TWIA has your claim and the information it requested, it generally has 60 days to accept or deny it (Tex. Ins. Code §2210.573).</p></div>
<p>If you're on the coast, check which company covers wind and hail. Many coastal homeowners have a private policy for everything else and a separate TWIA policy for wind and hail, and the TWIA deadline applies to the hail claim even if the private policy says something different.</p>

<h2>Private policies: find the deadline in two places</h2>
<p>For a private homeowners policy, open the full policy (not just the declarations page) and find these two conditions. Their exact titles vary by company, but they're usually in the "Conditions" section.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>Duties after loss.</strong> This lists what you must do after damage, starting with notice to the insurer. Many Texas forms say "prompt notice." Some add a specific outer limit for wind and hail, commonly one year after the date of loss.</li>
<li><strong>Suit against us.</strong> This sets how long you have to file a lawsuit against the insurer, and from what starting point. Texas law generally doesn't allow a contract to cut the time to sue below two years, but the way your policy measures the start date matters, so read it closely.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check any endorsements too. A wind and hail endorsement can change the reporting deadline in the main form. If you can't find the wording, ask your agent or the insurer for a complete certified copy of the policy in effect on the storm date.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Don't rely on a general rule:</strong> "two years" is not a safe assumption for reporting a hail claim on a private Texas policy. If your policy has a one-year reporting limit for wind and hail, a claim filed in month thirteen can be denied on that basis alone.</p></div>

<h2>What "prompt notice" means in practice</h2>
<p>A policy that requires prompt notice doesn't give you a number of days. It means reporting within a reasonable time after you knew, or should have known, about the damage. A homeowner who reports a week after a widely reported storm is clearly prompt. One who saw dented gutters in April and reported in December will have to explain the gap.</p>
<p>Late notice causes two problems even when you're inside any hard deadline:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The insurer may argue it couldn't inspect fresh damage.</strong> The claim file will note how long you waited, and an adjuster may attribute what they find to later storms or ordinary wear.</li>
<li><strong>Water damage that spread during the delay may be excluded.</strong> Most policies require you to protect the property from further damage. If a leak ran for months, the insurer may pay for the roof and refuse the rotted decking, stained ceilings or mold that followed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The fix is simple: report as soon as you see signs of hail damage, even if you're not sure how bad it is. Reporting is not the same as demanding a roof. If the adjuster finds nothing, the claim closes without payment.</p>

<h2>Old hail damage: the storm-date problem</h2>
<p>Hail damage often goes unnoticed for months. Bruised shingles don't always leak right away, and nobody looks at their roof until a roofer, a home inspector, or a ceiling stain points it out. Insurance can still cover older hail damage if it happened while the policy was in force and you report it within the policy's limits. The hard part is proof.</p>
<p>To pay, the insurer needs to tie the damage to a specific storm on a specific date. Texas often gets several hail days a year in the same area, and a roof can carry hits from more than one. Here's what makes an older claim stronger:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Find the storm.</strong> Use our <a href="/tools/storm-report-lookup/">storm report lookup</a> to pull NOAA reports for your address. Look for a date with hail of a size consistent with what's on your roof.</li>
<li><strong>Match the damage to the storm.</strong> Damage on the slopes facing the storm's direction, dents in soft metals of a consistent size, and fresh-looking fractures all help. Our <a href="/post/how-to-identify-hail-damage-by-roof-type/">guide to hail damage by roof type</a> explains what fresh and weathered hits look like.</li>
<li><strong>Use dated evidence.</strong> A home inspection report, a listing photo, or a roofer's invoice from before the storm that shows the roof in good shape is valuable. So are your own phone photos with dates.</li>
<li><strong>Check prior claims.</strong> If an earlier claim on the same roof was paid and not repaired, the insurer may refuse to pay for the same damage twice. Be ready to show what was fixed.</li>
</ol>
<p>The date of loss you report also decides which policy period and which deductible apply. If you switched insurers, the storm date determines which company owes the claim. Pick the date from evidence, not convenience.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>After you file: the insurer's deadlines</h2>
<p>Once you report a claim to a private Texas insurer, Chapter 542 of the Insurance Code puts the insurer on a clock.</p>
<div class="keyfacts">
<div><span>15 days</span><p>to acknowledge, begin investigating and request needed items</p></div>
<div><span>15 business days</span><p>to accept or reject after it has everything it asked for</p></div>
<div><span>5 business days</span><p>to pay after notifying you of acceptance</p></div>
</div>
<p>If the insurer needs more time to decide, it must tell you why, and then decide within 45 days of that notice. After a weather-related catastrophe declared by the commissioner, each deadline is extended by 15 days. Surplus lines carriers get longer periods (30 business days to acknowledge, 20 business days to pay). When an insurer pays late, Texas law adds penalty interest and attorney's fees to what it owes.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/texas-claim-deadline-calculator/">Texas claim deadline calculator</a> turns your claim dates into the specific due dates. For what to do when an adjuster doesn't call, see <a href="/post/how-long-does-an-insurance-adjuster-have-to-contact-you/">how long an insurance adjuster has to contact you</a>.</p>

<h2>Deadlines that apply later in the claim</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Report a TWIA claim</td><td>1 year from date of damage (extendable up to 180 days for good cause)</td><td>Tex. Ins. Code §2210.573</td></tr>
<tr><td>Report a private-policy claim</td><td>Prompt notice; often an outer limit such as one year for wind and hail</td><td>Your policy's duties after loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Collect recoverable depreciation</td><td>Repairs completed within the time the policy allows</td><td>Your policy's loss settlement section</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notice before a lawsuit</td><td>Written notice at least 61 days before suit on a weather claim</td><td>Tex. Ins. Code §542A.003</td></tr>
<tr><td>File suit</td><td>Set by the "Suit against us" clause; generally not less than two years</td><td>Your policy, read with Texas law</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The recoverable depreciation deadline catches people who settle the claim and then wait a year to replace the roof. If your policy pays replacement cost, find out how long you have to finish repairs and send the final invoice, and ask the insurer in writing for an extension before that time runs out if your roofer is backed up. The 61-day pre-suit notice matters only if the claim is heading toward litigation, but it means an attorney should be involved well before the suit deadline, not in the final month.</p>

<h2>If you think you've missed the deadline</h2>
<p>Don't assume the claim is dead. Read the exact policy wording first; the deadline may run from a different date than you think, or apply only to certain kinds of damage. Report the claim anyway with the best storm-date evidence you have and let the insurer respond in writing. If it denies on timing alone, that letter is something an attorney can review. Our guide to a <a href="/post/denied-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">denied hail damage claim</a> covers the steps after a denial, and <a href="/post/when-is-it-too-late-to-hire-a-public-adjuster/">when it's too late to hire a public adjuster</a> explains when we can still help.</p>
